hese devices we  don't want them to think they're using a  web browser cos they probably start  using it for some completely unrelated  purpose so we want them to just see this  as being you know and regular native app  that's the only thing that they can run  on that device it's very easy using PWA  api's to make your application  installable all you need is a manifest  file and i've got one here it's very  simple it's gives the name of the  application an icon and some colors and  I just need to register that in my  index.html so I'm going to add this link  tag right here that points to this  manifest file let's see what effect that  has now before I reload I want you to  watch very carefully these icons on the  top right up here you see I've got two  icons  we've got zoom and we've got favourites  but when I reload because of my manifest  hopefully a third icon will now become  available  what's this extra little plus thing  that's appeared well that now says  install blazer Mart and the user or your  sis ID mineral ever can now install this  application onto the device and if they  do that it no longer shows up in the  browser but it now pops up in its own  window without any other browser UI  around it but it's still the real  application and it  and the user can do things like find it  on their Start menu so if I start  searching you'll see I've got blazer  Mart here that's installed click that  pops up in its own window and it's still  the real thing it's gonna do all the  stuff that it was doing before okay very  good so hopefully that gives you a sense  of some of the nice things that we can  do with a blazer application that's  running on webassembly what we've done  is we've been able to use G RPC to get  strongly-typed data access with an  efficient efficient network traffic  we've been able to write some unit tests  in quite a nice expressive way and we've  been able to take advantage of running  inside the client to use some of these  PW our features ok now let's change the  subject slightly now and let's think  about this some slightly broader topics  I want to think about all the ways that  we can run a blazer application you may  remember from the start of this talk  that we had this slide before which  talked about client side blazer and  server side blazer well let's just think  about these two models a little bit and  why you would choose between each of  them in fact can you even use these are  these released and supported yet well  when it comes to server side blazer that  has been shipped already that is part of  the espenak core 3 release that's  already been out for about a month so  that is fully released fully supported  ready for your production use today  client side blazer or blazer webassembly  on the other hand that is still in  preview we've committed to shipping it  but we haven't done so yet and we've got  a targeted release date of May next year  so you've got about 6 months to wait  until that becomes a production  supported thing it has been in preview  for a long time many many people have  been using it successfully already many  people have been putting it into  production already even though we tell  them not to but it's up to you you can  go and have a go with that and hopefully  you'll find that it's nice and  successful but again I want to emphasize  what I see as being the main point here  which is that it's flexible if you build  some application using blazer then you  can run it as client-side you can run it  as server-side you can change between  them you can use birth at once whatever  it's up to you you get that kind of  flexibility but still you might be  thinking all right thanks for the  flexibility but what do  even wonderful why would I use one or  the other well some of the benefits of  using client-side laser are that because  it's a true client-side application it  will be able to work offline it's truly  in the browser it can use all the PWA  features and it's just there you know  running on the client it means that  you're not having to spend any of your  server resources on the CPU and RAM  needed to run it it's just there in the  browser and because it's right there  when it wants to update the UI it does  so with zero latency so you can write  really really high performance rapidly  updating you eyes that update instantly  inside the browser that another cool  thing about blades are running on the  client is that when it comes to  deploying it as far as your web server  is concerned it's just static files you  don't you can serve it from an ASP  nightcore application but you don't  actually need any dotnet on the server  all if you wanted to you could serve it  from a node.js application you could put  it on a CDN you could serve it from as  your static web sites or something and  just to really prove that to you I've  deployed my blazer Mart application to  get hub pages you probably know github  pages is a thing that github gives you  that lets you deploy static sites and  here it is  so here's blazer Mart and you can see  it's running from this github do I owe  you RL but it's still the same  application that we had before it still  does all the same things that you  expected ok so that means we've got this  very very cheap and fast geographically  distributed hosting available very  easily ok that's good what about on the  other side then what are the benefits of  writing server-side Blaser well one of  the major drawbacks of client-side  blazer is that it does involve bringing  this dotnet runtime down into the  browser and at the moment the default  blazer application is two point four  megabytes transferred over the wire so  that's quite big we're working on making  it smaller but still it's quite big and  also it requires using the user CPU to  execute server-side laser on the other  hand is a thin client model so that's  putting all the load onto the server  instead which means that it's going to  work even on super low-end devices it's  going to load just about instantly even  own slower network connections and the  end users device doesn't have to do the  work to update the UI because the server  will do it ok now another cool thing  about server-side blazer is because your  code is on the server  you can do everything that server-side  dotnet core can do including things like  interact with arbitrary bits of hardware  on the server so imagine that you want  to do some super advanced GPU powered  machine learning thing well that will  work incredibly easily with suicide  laser you're already running on the  server you can just use whatever kind of  cloud based GPUs you want and it's all  just going to execute there and send the  results down to the browser wonderful  and you don't even have to disclose your  proprietary data models it all just  stays up there in the cloud so that's  another major one of the benefits of  server-side Blaser of obviously the main  drawback to server-side laser is because  you're running in the cloud all of the  interactions are happening place  happening over the network so if there's  a lot of latency in the between the  client and the server end users will see  that latency so we recommend that for a  really good user experience you need to  distribute your servers such that each  user is within about 100 milliseconds of  round-trip time to the server okay right  so those are the current ways of running  a blazer application that's what we've  committed to ship so far but what about  further down the line are there other  ways that we can run blazer well if you  came to Ryan's talk this morning you  will have heard about the idea of blazer  and electron I'm not going to demo that  for you because that was already demoed  today but that allows you to take an  electron shell to run a blazer  application as a desktop application  without any webassembly involved so it's  using regular net core on the desktop to  render the application and use al